  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 250 PM EDT Monday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Hot conditions prevail during the early to middle \nportion of the week. Isolated storms possible, but mostly dry. \n\nSfc high pressure remains near the area this afternoon with temps in \nthe upper 80s to around 90. Observational data shows a corridor of \nlocally higher temperatures across interior srn VA up into the \nNorthern Neck, which is also in the vicinity of a weak wind shift. \nAlready am seeing a few showers popping up in this area and could \nsee some additional isolated activity through the early evening \nhours. This is in agreement with some of the latest CAMs. Another \narea of showers (and perhaps a rumble of thunder) is developing \non the lower MD Eastern Shore. \n\nOtherwise, a late-summer heat wave is imminent as a strong upper \nlevel ridge building over the Mississippi River valley translates \ntoward the east coast by the mid-late week period. Temperatures rise \nthrough the week and peak near 100 on Wednesday and Thursday. If \nrealized, these temps would likely break a few max temp records for \nearly September. Unfortunately, it will also feel humid and stagnant \non top of the already hot conditions with little to no wind and dew \npoints in the upper 60s and low 70s. Heat indices in the upper 90s \nand low 100s are expected today and Tues, then 102-107 on Wed/Thurs. \nHeat Advisories are likely for at least part of the area these days; \nhighest confidence in this is E of I-95. Overnight lows will not \nprovide much relief, especially Wed night when lows will be in the \nupper 70s.\n\nDry conditions are generally expected Tuesday and Wednesday. \nHowever, given the NW flow aloft and no short supply of instability, \nstronger storms are possible, but confidence is pretty low at the \nmoment. Any convective activity would likely be in the form of \nleftover MCS activity from our N, the evolution of which are \nrelatively difficult to predict with much lead time. CAMs depict\nvarious solutions, ranging from dry to large thunderstorm\ncomplexes. SPC has N/NE portions of the area in a Marginal Risk\nfor Tuesday and a sliver of Marginal/Slight Risks for the lower\nMD Eastern Shore Wednesday. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Shower/storm chances increase late week as a cold \nfront approaches. Cooler, but seasonable, temps forecast behind the \nfront by the weekend. \n\nThe strong ridge aloft starts to break down/flatten late in the \nweek. Meanwhile, a sfc cold front approaches from the north, most \nlikely passing through the area sometime Friday. Afternoon and \nevening storms appear quite probable Friday with the front around, \nbut will also be possible Thursday, similar to the setups of Tuesday \nand Wednesday. NW flow will remain place aloft and this flow \nwill progressively strengthen as the associated trough drops \nsouthward along the NE/Mid-Atlantic coast. All in all, the \ncombination of instability and this increasing flow (and shear) \nsuggests any storms could be strong-severe. SPC has outlooked \nareas N of the NC/VA border in a severe risk for Thursday and \nFriday. \n\nWe should see a little bit of relief from the heat over the weekend, \nbut still seasonably warm with highs in the low 80s. The chance of \nshowers/storms will also be lower, but not zero.",
